1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,283
In 1806 a young man from Boston tried to
sell frozen pond water on a tropical
island,

2
00:00:06,283 --> 00:00:08,028
and everyone laughed at him,

3
00:00:08,028 --> 00:00:12,827
but thirty years later he was shipping ice
halfway around the world to India.

4
00:00:13,277 --> 00:00:15,037
His name was Frederic Tudor,

5
00:00:15,037 --> 00:00:18,621
and a Boston newspaper joked that it was a
slippery plan,

6
00:00:18,621 --> 00:00:21,261
because most of the ice melted on the way,

7
00:00:21,261 --> 00:00:24,342
and Martinique had nowhere to keep the
rest cold.

8
00:00:24,792 --> 00:00:26,607
So he lost thousands of dollars,

9
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:30,010
and it got worse until his debts put him
in debtors' prison,

10
00:00:30,010 --> 00:00:31,598
but he still didn't give up.

11
00:00:32,048 --> 00:00:36,655
Because he worked out that a big block
melts far more slowly than small pieces,

12
00:00:36,655 --> 00:00:38,288
so he cut his blocks square,

13
00:00:38,288 --> 00:00:42,370
packed them tight and buried them in
sawdust that sawmills threw away.

14
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:49,489
So in 1833 he loaded 180 tons of pond ice
onto a ship called the Tuscany,

15
00:00:49,489 --> 00:00:53,042
and after four months at sea, crossing the
equator twice,

16
00:00:53,042 --> 00:00:56,221
about 100 tons were still frozen in
Calcutta.

17
00:00:56,671 --> 00:01:01,013
And people there were so amazed that they
built a house just for his ice,

18
00:01:01,013 --> 00:01:03,155
and he became known as the Ice King.

19
00:01:03,605 --> 00:01:08,025
So every clink of ice in a glass goes back
to a trip that went completely wrong.
